                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Correct. The rate law can be experimentally determined by changing the initial concentration of each reactant and observing the change in the reaction rate. If the concentration of a reactant is increased by a factor of two (doubled) and the observed reaction rate increases by a factor of four, then the order of the reaction (the exponent) for that reactant must be 2: doubling the concentration means doubling the rate, 2"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "2"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " = 4. If the concentration of a reactant is tripled and the observed reaction rate also increases by a factor of three, then order of the reaction (the exponent) for that reactant must be 1, since 3"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "1"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " = 3."
